                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Differences from Xortex:\n- Larger playspace requiring more movement\n- Enemies attacking from behind (forward/360 setting didn't seem to change anything)\n- You don't die from one hit\n\nFirst of all, if you liked Xortex, you should give this game a chance. I, however, enjoyed Xortex for a while but this game didn't entertain me. Why? Who knows. Maybe this type of game just bores me quickly.\nProblems:\n- Runs relatively poorly and looks bad. Had noticeable slowdowns at gr8 graphics setting with i7 @ 4,4GHz and OC'd GTX 1080. Considering how the game looks, this shouldn't happen.\n- There are different enemies but all you can really do against all of them is to point and shoot. You don't even prioritize certain types like in Xortex.\n- You have to twist your arm in weird positions to kill the enemies chasing you.\n- My highscore didn't update to the leaderboards (might have been due to windows firewall - after I stopped playing I noticed it was asking for permission). After restarting the game score was wiped (upgrades stayed).\n- The game is very easy after you buy the active repair skill. I didn't come even close to dying so I suicided at wave 34. Difficulty setting doesn't seem to make much of a difference, at least at start.\n- You have to press the trigger to fire. I found this unpleasant. Especially considering that you have no reason to stop firing.\n\nConclusion: Ugly and poorly balanced shooter that you might enjoy nonetheless.",
